in a city built on creating drama even a  burst water main it seems has to be just  that bit more spectacular in the middle  of Sunset Boulevard at one point water  shooting from the ruptured pipeline at  36,000 gallons a minute the road surface  unable to cope under the pressure the  only place for that water to go was  downhill and onto the campus of UCLA  flooding college grounds and pouring  into buildings students there captured  these images of the water surging into  an underground carpark and on roads  nearby cars stranded in the floodwater  a number of people were rescued from  vehicles as the level rose the sheer  speed with which it happened left even  fire crews struggling to cope and some  students decided it was too good an  opportunity to waste their boogie boards  put to good use the university's sports  fields were left looking more like lakes  and in the middle of rush hour part of a  city notorious for its congestion  brought to a standstill it took hours  for crews to shut off the water worried  that doing it too quickly would burst  pipes elsewhere the assessments of how  much it will cost to repair the damage  have only just begun  in the midst of their worst drought for  decades Californians have been desperate  for some relief this isn't what they  needed Gregg Milam Sky News Los Angeles
 

